    "body": "UPS has sued the federal government to recover tariffs the Supreme Court struck down in February. Much of the money would go to customers who paid the duties.\nWhat’s Related\nUPS is asking the U.S. Court of International Trade to order U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) to reopen certain import entries and refund the duties, with interest. The tariffs were imposed under the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA), and the Supreme Court ruled on Feb. 20 that the law didn’t give the president the power to impose them.\nThe case covers import entries for which Customs had already made a final decision on the duties owed, including entries subject to a protest. UPS was the importer of record on those entries, meaning it was the party responsible for the goods when they cleared Customs.\nWhy UPS needs a judge\nCBP has been paying refunds in phases through its online system, known as CAPE. The first phase opened April 20 and covered entries Customs hadn’t finalized yet, plus some it had closed only recently.\nOn Oct. 6, CBP opened a third phase for entries where the duties were already final. There’s a catch, though. Those entries qualify only if the importer has a case at the Court of International Trade and a court order telling Customs to reopen them. UPS is asking for that order.\nRELATED: Why Retailers Are Losing So Much Money on Returns\nBreaking down UPS' payments to customers\nUPS received about $500 million in the first refund round. By Sept. 30, it had passed about $230 million along to customers and still had about $270 million to distribute, based on figures in its third-quarter filing.\nUPS says its customers paid the tariffs in many cases. Customs sends the refund to UPS as importer of record, and UPS matches each entry to the original payment before passing the money along. It plans to do the same with whatever it recovers through the lawsuit.\nHow customers get paid\nFor first-phase refunds, UPS first applies the refund and any applicable interest to past-due invoices on the account. It then sends any remaining balance to the customer.\nShippers can see which of their shipments qualify using UPS’s online refund lookup tool. You can log in to see everything tied to your account, or search by tracking number or delivery address. The tool shows each shipment’s refund phase, status and amount. Entries marked “future phase” could be part of a later CBP refund round.",
